# Ignore rules for THIS Dockerfile only.
#
# BuildKit prefers `<dockerfile>.dockerignore` over the context's .dockerignore,
# which is the whole reason this file exists: the root .dockerignore whitelists
# only docs/ and mkdocs.yml for the docs image. Adding hermes/ and workflows/
# there would drag both into the docs build context for no reason.
#
# Whitelist, same shape as the root file — the repository root also holds .git
# and workflows/.venv, which together dwarf everything this image needs.
*
!hermes/
!workflows/
!docker/hermes-init/entrypoint.sh
!docker/hermes-init/migrate.sh
!docker/hermes-init/marker.sh
!docker/hermes-init/migrations/

# Re-exclude the expensive and the pointless from the paths admitted above.
# Virtualenvs and caches are hundreds of megabytes and are rebuilt inside the
# workflows image; tests and eval datasets are not needed to RUN a workflow, and
# the datasets carry sample contact data that has no business on a customer's
# disk.
#
# The patterns are deliberately wider than the two names that exist today.
# `workflows/.venv/` alone was the rule, and it let a local build through at
# 1.27GB against CI's 22MB — the difference being `workflows/.uv-cache/` (955MB)
# and `workflows/.venv-kanban-test/`, neither of which existed when the rule was
# written. CI never saw it because a fresh checkout has no caches, so the only
# person who ever hit it was someone building by hand, and what they shipped was
# their own uv cache inside a customer image.
workflows/.venv*/
workflows/.uv-cache/
workflows/.google-agents-cli/
workflows/adk-state/
workflows/tests/
**/.venv/
**/*.cache/
**/.cache/
**/__pycache__/
**/*.pyc
**/.pytest_cache/
**/.ruff_cache/
